For an incompressible flow, [latex]\nabla \cdot \vec{V}=0[/latex] Given; the velocity field of a two-dimensional, incompressible flow, [latex]\vec{V}=2 \sinh x \hat{i}+v(x, y) \hat{j} \& \quad v(x, 0)=\cosh x[/latex] Now, [latex]\nabla \cdot \vec{V}=0[/latex] (for an incompressible flow) i.e. [latex]\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}=0[/latex] [latex]
2 \cosh x+\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}=0
[/latex] [latex]\Rightarrow \quad \partial \mathbf{v}=-2 \cosh \mathrm{x} \cdot \partial \mathrm{y}[/latex] Integrate both sides, [latex]
\begin{aligned}
& \int \partial v=-\int 2 \cosh x \cdot \partial y \\
& v=-2 y \cosh x+C  \;\;\;...(1)
\end{aligned}
[/latex] Given; [latex]\mathrm{v}(\mathrm{x}, 0)=\cosh \mathrm{x}[/latex] [latex]\therefore \quad \cosh x=-2(0) \cosh x+C[/latex] [latex]\Rightarrow \mathrm{C}=\cosh \mathrm{x}[/latex] Now, from equation (1) [latex]
\begin{aligned}
v(x, y) & =-2 y \cosh x+\cosh x \\
& =(1-2 y) \cosh x
\end{aligned}
[/latex] At [latex](0,-1)[/latex] [latex]
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{v}(0,-1) & =[1-2(-1)] \cosh (0) \\
& =3
\end{aligned}
[/latex]

